<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Rai dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">489 CE – 632 CE (approx.) · capital Aror</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A dynasty of Sindh known from the Chach Nama, which ruled the lower Indus from Aror before the Brahman dynasty took power.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Rai dynasty is known almost entirely from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chach-nama">Chach Nama</a>, a Persian history of the Arab conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> written in the 13th century from earlier Arabic sources. It describes a line of five kings ruling Sindh from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aror">Aror</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukkur">Sukkur</a>, from around the end of the 5th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chach-nama">Chach Nama</a> portrays a large kingdom stretching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir's</a> borders to the sea and from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/makran-state">Makran</a> to the edge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, divided into provinces under governors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was widespread in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> in this period, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> confirms, and the dynasty was reportedly Buddhist or tolerant of Buddhism. One king is said to have died fighting an army from Sasanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chach-nama">Chach Nama</a>, after the death of the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rai-sahasi-ii">Rai Sahasi II</a>, his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> chamberlain <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-chach">Chach</a> married the widowed queen and seized the throne around 632, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/brahman-dynasty-of-sindh">Brahman dynasty of Sindh</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 489 CE – 632 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:69.43%;width:2.85%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>489 CE – 632 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aror</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sindhi Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Pakistan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rai dynasty of Sindh, Rai kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>
